Q: Using CSS linear-gradient in SVG A: <p><strong>TL;DR: you can't apply CSS gradients to SVG child elements</strong></p>
<p>For the sake of brevity I use shortened terms: By SVG gradients I refer to SVG's descriptive elements <code>&lt;linearGradient&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;radialGradient&gt;</code>.
By CSS gradients I mean all kind of <code>background</code> properties related CSS gradients.</p>
<p>As commented by <em>Robert Longson</em> it is just impossible – the same applies to SVG gradients that can't be applied to HTML elements.`</p>
<p>Simplified: SVG and CSS specs evolve separately and in the end it's about the vendors whether they implement specified features. Many CSS features, such as animations, »clip-path« and color gradients, were already available in SVG from the very beginning – long before the concepts of the CSS module were introduced. On the other hand later CSS concepts often supercede SVG equivalents in terms of capabilities – for instance conic gradients. Ultimately, we are faced with »incompatible« design concepts that we must find a workaround.</p>
<h3>SVG/HTML gradients in 2026 (what we <em>can</em> do)</h3>
<p>This summary makes no claim to completeness and can hopefully be expanded in the future.</p>
<h4>CSS gradients in SVG foreignObject</h4>
<p>This works as long as you define an appropriate HTML namespace for the foreignObject content.
See <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG/Reference/Element/foreignObject" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mdn example</a></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>svg {
  display: block;
  outline: 1px solid #ccc;
  overflow: visible;
}

/* foreign object */
.grad-svg-foreign {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background: linear-gradient(to right, white, black);
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"&gt;
  &lt;foreignObject x="0" y="0" width="100" height="100"&gt;
    &lt;div class="grad-svg-foreign" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/foreignObject&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h4>CSS gradients applied to outermost SVG</h4>
<p>The outermost parent SVG behaves like a HTML element – you can apply pretty much any CSS property which would be ignored for SVG child elements.
This also includes <code>conic-gradients</code> which don't have an equivalent in SVG.</p>
<p>Worth noting: if the main goal is to apply a gradient to a complex vector shape – we may as well apply a  <code>clip-path</code> or <code>mask</code> to a HTML element.</p>
<p>This trick doesn't work for nested <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> elements as these behave as wrapping elements which don't have a default background rectangle but can inherit styles to child elements (similar to <code>&lt;g&gt;</code> group wrappers) or be manipulated via position properties (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>transform</code>).</p>
<p>So when we apply a SVG gradient to a parent SVG or a nested one – this style rule will only affect child elements.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>svg {
  display: block;
  outline: 1px solid #ccc;
  overflow: visible;
}

/* svg parent */
.svg-parent {
  /* apply to parent SVG */
  background: conic-gradient(red, orange, yellow, green, blue);

  /* clip to shape */
  clip-path: url('#clip1');

  /* inherit to child elements */
  fill: url('#svgGradExt2');
}

/* svg nested */
svg svg {
  fill: url(#svgGradExt)
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- hidden clip path def asset --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 1 1" style="width:0;height:0; position:absolute;"&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;clipPath id="clip1" clipPathUnits="objectBoundingBox"&gt;
      &lt;path d="M 0.5 0 a 0.5 0.5 0 0 1  0 1 0.5 0.5 0 0 1  0 -1" /&gt;
    &lt;/clipPath&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;

&lt;!-- hidden gradient def asset --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" style="width:0;height:0; position:absolute;"&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGradExt"&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="white" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="red" offset="100%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGradExt2" &gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="blue" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="orange" offset="50%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;

&lt;!-- visible svg --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="svg-parent" &gt;
  &lt;!-- nested svg: can only inherit styles --&gt;
  &lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" x="0" y="0" width="50" height="50" &gt;
    &lt;rect width="100" height="100" /&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;

&lt;!-- shows inherited gradient --&gt;
&lt;rect x="50" y="50" width="100" height="100" /&gt;

&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h4>Reference SVG gradients via ID in CSS</h4>
<p>Works provided your SVG gradient definition is inlined in HTM document DOM.
You can also move gradient definitions to a separate inlined SVG.
But you need to avoid duplicate IDs otherwise the browser will take the 1st occurence of a gradient.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>svg {
  display: block;
  outline: 1px solid #ccc;
  overflow: visible;
}

/* svg child rect */
.svg-rect{
  fill: url('#svgGrad');
}

.svg-rect-ext{
  fill: url('#svgGradExt');
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- hidden gradient def asset --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" style="width:0;height:0; position:absolute;"&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGradExt" data-gradientUnits="objectBoundingBox"&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="white" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="red" offset="100%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;

&lt;!-- ignored gradient def --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" style="width:0;height:0; position:absolute;"&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGradExt" data-gradientUnits="objectBoundingBox"&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="white" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="green" offset="100%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;


&lt;!-- visible svg --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100"&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGrad"&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="white" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop stop-color="black" offset="100%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;

  &lt;!-- svg child --&gt;
  &lt;rect class="svg-rect" x="50" y="0" width="50" height="50" /&gt;

  &lt;!-- svg child external reference --&gt;
  &lt;rect class="svg-rect-ext" x="0" y="50" width="50" height="50" /&gt;

&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h4>Manipulate SVG gradients via CSS</h4>
<p>Unfortunately, we can only access a limited amount of gradient properties via CSS – mostly color related ones such as <code>stop-color</code> and <code>stop-opacity</code>.</p>
<p>We can neither control <code>offset</code> which affects the actual gradient distribution nor <code>gradientUnits</code> – so all in all we're missing a lot of attributes which are crucial for SVG gradients. See also <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/SVG/Tutorials/SVG_from_scratch/Gradients" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mdn summary</a></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>svg {
  display: block;
  outline: 1px solid #ccc;
  overflow: visible;
}


/* foreign object */
.svg-parent{
  /* inherit to child elements */
  //fill: url('#svgGradExt2');
}

.grad1{
    fill: url('#svgGradExt1');
}

.grad2{
    fill: url('#svgGradExt2');
}


/* change svg gradient stop colors */
.grad1-stop1{
  stop-color:cyan;
}
.grad1-stop2{
  stop-color:purple;
}

.grad2-stop1{
  stop-color:orange;
}
.grad2-stop2{
  stop-color:red;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" style="width:0;height:0; position:absolute;"&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGradExt1"&gt;
      &lt;stop class="grad1-stop1" stop-color="white" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop class="grad1-stop2" stop-color="red" offset="100%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id="svgGradExt2" &gt;
      &lt;stop class="grad2-stop1" stop-color="blue" offset="0%" /&gt;
      &lt;stop class="grad2-stop2" stop-color="orange" offset="50%" /&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;


&lt;!-- visible svg --&gt;
&lt;svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="svg-parent" &gt;
  &lt;rect class="grad1" x="0" y="0" width="50" height="50" /&gt;
  &lt;rect class="grad2" x="50" y="50" width="50" height="50" /&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h4>What about CSS <code>offset</code> property?</h4>
<p>Unfortunately, we have a naming collision between the SVG gradient related attribute <code>offset</code> and CSS <code>offset</code> which refers to the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/offset-path" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>offset-path</code></a> properties.</p>
<p>This is highly confusing as you won't see a hint in dev tools for using an invalid property.</p>
<p>All in all if you need all bells-and-whistles of CSS gradients you may try to use vector clipped HTML elements using <code>clip-path</code> or <code>mask</code> instead.</p>
<p><code>&lt;foreignObject&gt;</code> is also quite infamous for cross-browser rendering issues so to this date slightly wobbly.</p>

Q: How do I calculate CRC32 of a string A: <p>In modern <strong>.NET 6+ / .NET Core</strong>, the official and most efficient way to compute CRC32 is using the built-in <code>System.IO.Hashing</code> namespace:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System;
using System.Text;
using System.IO.Hashing;

string input = &quot;your string here&quot;;
byte[] bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input);

// Returns standard little-endian byte array
byte[] hash = Crc32.Hash(bytes);
uint crc = BitConverter.ToUInt32(hash, 0);

Console.WriteLine($&quot;{crc:X8}&quot;); // Formats as 8-digit hexadecimal
</code></pre>

Q: How to know when a page redirect or refresh action is cancelled, Javascript A: <p>We can solve this problem with a Service Worker. There are three cases we need to consider;</p>
<p><strong>Everything succeeds</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The browser will preallocate a new client id for the new page.</li>
<li>The fetch call will return a response object based on the server response headers.</li>
<li>The old page is unloaded.</li>
<li>The new page is created.</li>
<li>Any call to <code>clients.get(event.resultingClientId)</code> will resolve to the client api for the new page.</li>
<li>The browser will load the new content from the response.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The user aborts the navigation</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The browser deallocates the new client id, no new page is created.</li>
<li>Any call to <code>clients.get(event.resultingClientId)</code> will resolve to <code>undefined</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>There are discussions online suggesting that a future version may abort <code>event.request.signal</code> when this occurs. However, at the time of writiing no browser actually does this.</p>
<p><strong>The server returns a <code>ContentDisposition</code> header.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The fetch call will return a response object based on the server response headers.</li>
<li>The old page is left alone</li>
<li>The browser deallocates the new client id, no new page is created.</li>
<li>Any call to <code>clients.get(event.resultingClientId)</code> will resolve to <code>undefined</code>.</li>
<li>The browser continues to process the fetch response and downloads the file.</li>
</ul>
<p>Note that I have only confirmed this <code>clients.get()</code> behaviour in the current version of Chrome. I don't know what the standard is supposed to be.</p>
<p>Thankfully, we can boil the above down to a couple of simple rules.</p>
<p>If the original <code>event.request.signal</code> is aborted, and we aren't planning to read and cache the response. We should abort the signal we passed to <code>fetch()</code>.</p>
<p>When <code>clients.get(event.resultingClientId)</code> resolves to <code>undefined</code>, we should send a message to the originating client.</p>
<p>If the new client is <code>undefined</code>, <code>fetch</code> has not yet returned, and we do not intend to cache the response. We should abort the signal we passed to <code>fetch()</code>.</p>
<pre><code>self.addEventListener(&quot;fetch&quot;, event =&gt; {
    event.respondWith(async () =&gt; {
        var client = await self.clients.get(event.clientId);
        var returned = false;

        var controller = new AbortController();
        event.request.signal.addEventListener(&quot;abort&quot;, () =&gt; controller.abort());

        if (event.resultingClientId){
            self.clients.get(event.resultingClientId)
                .then(newClient =&gt; {
                    if (newClient === undefined) {
                        client.postMessage( { TODO });
                        if (!returned)
                            controller.abort();
                    }
                });
        }

        const response = await fetch(event.request, { signal: controller.signal });
        returned = true;
        return response;
    });
});
</code></pre>
<p>Adding a <code>try{} catch{}</code> left as an exercise for the reader.</p>

Q: How to interact with multiple tables in sql or codeigniter? A: <p>You don't need all of those <code>from()</code> calls. You should only have 1 table in your FROM clause which contains the parent table name; then <code>join()</code> the 3 child tables to that parent array. Since the original column name <code>name</code> from each child table will suffer from name collisions, give unique aliases to each column in the SELECT clause. The <code>get_where()</code> method can consolidate the <code>from()</code>, <code>where()</code>, and <code>get()</code> calls.</p>
<p>Model:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getResultsByLanguage(int $languageId): array
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;select('u.name university, d.name department, l.name language')
        -&gt;join('university u', 'r.university_id = u.id')
        -&gt;join('department d', 'r.department_id = d.id')
        -&gt;join('language l', 'r.language_id = l.id')
        -&gt;order_by('d.name')
        -&gt;get('result r', ['l.id' =&gt; $languageId])
        -&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Controller:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function listForLanguage(int $languageId)
{
    $data['results'] = $this-&gt;Academia_model-&gt;getResultsByLanguage($languageId);
    $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('list_results', $data);
}
</code></pre>
<p>View:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>foreach ($results as $row) {
    printf(
        '&lt;div&gt;%s : %s : %s&lt;/div&gt;',
        $row-&gt;university,
        $row-&gt;department,
        $row-&gt;language
    );
}
</code></pre>
